# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Code coverage profiles and other test artifacts
*.out
coverage.*
*.coverprofile
profile.cov

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
# vendor/

# Go workspace file
go.work
go.work.sum

# env files — covers .env, .env.local, .env.production, .envrc, etc.
# .env.example stays tracked so contributors see the expected keys.
.env
.env.*
.envrc
!.env.example

# Karakuri runtime artifacts
bin/
karakuri.db
karakuri-obs/

# Frontend build output + deps. web/dist is embedded by web/embed.go at compile
# time (`make web-build` populates it; CI builds it before any Go job), so it is
# a build artifact rather than source.
#
# The placeholder inside it is the exception, and is tracked. `//go:embed
# all:dist` is a COMPILE-time pattern: with the directory absent the whole
# module fails to build, which is what every fresh clone and every git worktree
# got. CI never noticed because its Build job downloads the real dist as an
# artifact from the frontend job. A worktree has neither that artifact nor a
# Node toolchain, so an agent working in one could not build the tree it was
# asked to change.
web/dist/*
!web/dist/index.html
web/node_modules/

# Editor/IDE
# .idea/
# .vscode/

# Playwright's own output. The report is a build artifact of a test run, not a
# thing to review in a diff.
web/playwright-report/
web/test-results/

# Worktrees the loop provisions for delivery objectives, and the local config
# that points a deployment at its own adapter instances and credentials env
# vars. Both live inside the tree the agent works in, so an objective running
# `git add -A` would otherwise commit them.
worktrees/
config/local.yaml
